{
  "dom_id": "step-q81708",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=-\\frac{4}{(2+t)^{2}}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, find the velocity (in $\\text{m}/\\text{s}$) of the particle at time $t.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{4}{(2+t)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2+t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{4}{2+0}+C \\\\ 2 & =2+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{4}{2+t}.$",
